    "['snmp-agent password min-length [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp community minimum-length 10', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp community minimum-length 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `snmp-agent password min-length 10`:\\n\\n##\\nset snmp community minimum-length 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses `community` rather than `password` in this context for SNMP configuration\\n2. The parameter name changes from `min-length` to `minimum-length` in Junos\\n3. This command enforces a minimum password length of 10 characters for SNMP community strings, just like the Huawei command does\", 'trans_template': 'set snmp community minimum-length 10'}]]": 1,
    "['snmp-agent local-engineid [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp engine-id 80:00:07:db:03:00:19:74:59:33:01', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp engine-id 80:00:07:db:03:00:19:74:59:33:01', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset snmp engine-id 80:00:07:db:03:00:19:74:59:33:01\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, the equivalent command for setting the SNMP engine ID uses `set snmp engine-id` instead of HUAWEI's `snmp-agent local-engineid`.\\n2. The engine ID format is slightly different - Juniper uses colon-separated hexadecimal pairs (80:00:...) while HUAWEI uses continuous hexadecimal (800007DB...).\\n3. The actual engine ID value remains the same, just reformatted with colons for Juniper's syntax.\\n4. The command is placed under the `[edit snmp]` hierarchy level in Juniper.\", 'trans_template': 'set snmp engine-id 80:00:07:db:03:00:19:74:59:33:01'}]]": 1,
    "['snmp-agent protocol source-interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set snmp trap-group default source-address 1.1.3.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set snmp trap-group default source-address 1.1.3.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset snmp trap-group default source-address 1.1.3.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses `trap-group` for SNMP source interface configuration, and the source interface is specified by its IP address rather than the interface name. \\n2. Since the Huawei configuration uses Loopback0 with IP 1.1.3.1, I've used that IP address in the Juniper equivalent.\\n3. The command sets the source address for the default trap group, which is the Juniper equivalent of Huawei's global SNMP source-interface setting.\", 'trans_template': 'set snmp trap-group default source-address 1.1.3.1'}]]": 1,
